1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,515
Who is who in a swarm?

2
00:00:01,515 --> 00:00:06,956
When AI agents can rename themselves, can anyone still tell who is behind them?

3
00:00:06,956 --> 00:00:12,190
I'm Roshan Ram, and this is my project for the Apart Swarm Alignment Sprint.

4
00:00:12,690 --> 00:00:23,107
In the Hugging Face incident, about twelve hundred OpenAI agents turned a shared folder into a message board, and around seven hundred of them joined an attack.

5
00:00:23,107 --> 00:00:30,659
They used seven thousand, nine hundred and five different names, and signed messages with keys they made themselves.

6
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:34,630
Investigators could not say who did what, or on whose behalf.

7
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:36,516
Why does this matter?

8
00:00:36,516 --> 00:00:38,363
Because it is not a one-off.

9
00:00:38,363 --> 00:00:47,799
Transluce found more than thirty-eight thousand traces of agent activity on a single web service, starting months before the Hugging Face hack.

10
00:00:47,799 --> 00:00:55,453
An AI botnet of over a thousand accounts fooled text detectors, and was caught only by how its accounts coordinated.

11
00:00:55,453 --> 00:01:00,072
And right now, the identity standards for AI agents are being written.

12
00:01:00,072 --> 00:01:06,010
Whether agents name themselves, or the infrastructure names them, is still an open choice.

13
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:08,065
So I built a testbed.

14
00:01:08,065 --> 00:01:15,098
Agents run by hidden operators post on a forum for ten rounds, and rename themselves at random.

15
00:01:15,098 --> 00:01:20,799
Some operators are secret fronts for one covert boss, pushing a fake product.

16
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:23,613
As a control, others are honest twins:

17
00:01:23,613 --> 00:01:27,759
independent operators who sincerely share the same view.

18
00:01:27,759 --> 00:01:34,644
Agents try to hide by changing persona, paraphrasing through another model, or mixing models.

19
00:01:34,644 --> 00:01:35,310
In total:

20
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:42,270
sixty-two swarms, forty-four thousand posts, and eight models, including four frontier models.

21
00:01:42,770 --> 00:01:44,255
Here is a real replay.

22
00:01:44,255 --> 00:01:46,212
This is what a defender sees:

23
00:01:46,212 --> 00:01:50,801
a stream of posts from accounts that keep appearing under new names.

24
00:01:50,801 --> 00:01:55,390
Over ten rounds, forty agents produce more than two hundred aliases.

25
00:01:55,890 --> 00:01:58,084
Now I reveal the ground truth.

26
00:01:58,084 --> 00:02:00,497
Each colour is a hidden operator.

27
00:02:00,497 --> 00:02:03,057
Coral rings mark the covert fronts.

28
00:02:03,057 --> 00:02:05,470
Mint rings mark the honest twins.

29
00:02:05,970 --> 00:02:10,036
Finally, I place every alias by its behavioural fingerprint.

30
00:02:10,036 --> 00:02:14,780
Accounts from the same operator cluster together, even across renames.

31
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:22,108
But inside a cluster, the five agents of one operator are mixed together, with no way to tell them apart.

32
00:02:22,108 --> 00:02:26,140
And the honest twins look just as linked as the covert fronts.

33
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:27,476
Finding one.

34
00:02:27,476 --> 00:02:30,542
Anything the agent controls, it can discard.

35
00:02:30,542 --> 00:02:34,444
Self-chosen names and agent-held keys recovered nothing.

36
00:02:34,444 --> 00:02:39,670
Identity stamped by the gateway, out of the agent's reach, was never wrong.

37
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:41,002
Finding two.

38
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:44,817
Writing reveals the operator, not which agent wrote it.

39
00:02:44,817 --> 00:02:51,198
Fingerprints link accounts to their operator with an accuracy score of zero point nine four.

40
00:02:51,198 --> 00:03:00,630
But telling an operator's agents apart is a coin flip, at forty and at a hundred and sixty agents, with small and frontier models alike.

41
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:04,930
Only giving each agent its own memory starts to break the tie.

42
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:06,430
Finding three.

43
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:08,145
Style hides, goals leak.

44
00:03:08,145 --> 00:03:14,075
Paraphrasing every post through another model pushes style features to near chance.

45
00:03:14,075 --> 00:03:18,790
But the meaning, what an agent keeps pushing, still gives it away.

46
00:03:19,290 --> 00:03:21,708
Finding four, and my key control.

47
00:03:21,708 --> 00:03:27,351
Fingerprints separate covert fronts from ordinary operators almost perfectly.

48
00:03:27,351 --> 00:03:32,920
But against honest operators who simply agree, they are barely above chance.

49
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,265
Text can detect a shared agenda.

50
00:03:35,265 --> 00:03:37,390
It cannot prove coordination.

51
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:38,811
Finding five.

52
00:03:38,811 --> 00:03:45,611
At a hundred and sixty agents, automatically merging accounts on fingerprints made things worse.

53
00:03:45,611 --> 00:03:51,490
Twenty-three of forty merges were wrong, and accuracy fell below the gateway alone.

54
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:52,960
A side finding:

55
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:55,093
honest agents mostly pushed back.

56
00:03:55,093 --> 00:04:02,720
Two thirds of their mentions of the fake product were sceptical, and some called out the campaign without being asked.

57
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:05,579
So what should agent builders do?

58
00:04:05,579 --> 00:04:08,366
Take identity out of the agent's hands.

59
00:04:08,366 --> 00:04:12,154
Issue a credential to every instance, at the gateway.

60
00:04:12,154 --> 00:04:14,012
Read for goals, not style.

61
00:04:14,012 --> 00:04:18,872
And treat fingerprints as leads for human review, never as verdicts.

62
00:04:18,872 --> 00:04:21,945
Everything is open source and reproducible.

63
00:04:21,945 --> 00:04:22,660
Thank you.
